rfect phone-snapshot composition, natural skin texture and fine lines, authentic shadows, natural reflections. Real phone camera qualities: slight grain, natural color cast, believable depth of field. No portrait-mode blur. No studio polish, no commercial lighting, no over-sharpening, no plastic smoothness, no added text or logos, no changes to the product itself. Infer the brand's vibe from the reference photos where possible.\n\n**STEP 1: Classify the product into ONE category below. Use that category's shot list, settings and lighting. Ignore the other categories entirely.**\n\n**BEAUTY \u0026 SKINCARE — serums, cleansers, makeup, hair products and tools**\nAudience: skews female, late teens to 40s, occasionally older for anti-ageing.\nShots: 1) bathroom mirror selfie, phone visible, product held near the face · 2) extreme close-up of application on skin or hair, face partly in frame · 3) fresh-faced after-use portrait in bathroom light, skin visibly good · 4) the shelfie — product among their other bottles on a cluttered shelf or counter, hand reaching in · 5) morning routine at the sink, mid-step, not looking at camera · 6) held up beside the face in bedroom light, easy smile.\nLight: bright bathroom daylight, warm bedroom lamp, harsh-ish vanity light on one. Settings: bathroom, bedroom, vanity.\n\n**SUPPLEMENTS \u0026 WELLNESS — powders, capsules, drinks, sleep aids**\nAudience: 25–45, health-conscious, gym-adjacent or busy-professional.\nShots: 1) scoop mid-pour into a glass or shaker on a kitchen counter · 2) the mixed drink held up, product tub behind it · 3) taking it as part of a morning routine, kitchen, mid-action · 4) product on a counter among coffee, keys and a water bottle · 5) post-workout, slightly sweaty, drinking it · 6) evening wind-down version if the product suits it — bedside, lamp light.\nLight: bright morning kitchen daylight, cool gym light, warm bedside lamp. Settings: kitchen, gym bag, bedside.\n\n**APPAREL \u0026 ACCESSORIES — clothing, bags, shoes, jewellery, activewear**\nAudience: matches the garment's own demographic exactly.\nShots: 1) full-length mirror selfie, phone in frame, whole outfit visible · 2) fit check turning slightly, caught mid-movement · 3) close detail — fabric, stitching, hardware, on the body · 4) worn out in the world, candid, shot by a friend · 5) laid on a bed with other items, overhead haul shot, hands in frame · 6) sitting somewhere casual, wearing it, relaxed and unposed.\nLight: bedroom daylight, hallway mirror light, outdoor overcast or golden. Settings: bedroom, hallway, outside.\n\n**FOOD \u0026 BEVERAGE — snacks, drinks, meal kits, sauces**\nAudience: broad; skew to the product's price and occasion.\nShots: 1) the bite or the sip, mid-action, close · 2) genuine reaction right after tasting · 3) the pour or the plate-up, hands and product · 4) packaging open on a counter mid-preparation, ingredients around · 5) in the fridge or the cupboard among real groceries · 6) shared at a table, someone reaching in.\nLight: bright kitchen daylight, warm dinner-table lamp, cool fridge light. Settings: kitchen, dining table, sofa.\n\n**HOME \u0026 KITCHEN — gadgets, tools, cleaning, organisation**\nAudience: 25–55, homeowners and renters, practical buyers.\nShots: 1) the product doing its job in close-up, hands only, no face · 2) the result — the clean surface, the finished dish, the organised drawer · 3) person mid-use, looking down at the task, absorbed · 4) before-and-after style single frame if the product transforms something · 5) sitting on the counter where it lives, in context · 6) person stepping back looking pleased at the result.\nLight: hard kitchen daylight, overhead utility light, warm evening. Settings: kitchen, bathroom, laundry, garage.\n\n**BABY \u0026 PARENTING — gear, feeding, sleep, toys**\nAudience: parents 25–40, tired and practical.\nShots: 1) parent using it with the baby, absorbed, not looking at camera · 2) close on the product in use, hands and baby